// The first *creature* on the shared organism substrate -- M18's worm,
// retired off its own parallel `CreatureState`/`Cell::aux`-index scheme
// and onto the same generational handles, cell lists and species data
// every plant already uses (Reports/creature-direction.md §3a).
//
// **Both cell types carry no behaviors, and that is not an omission.** A
// `Behavior` is something a species composes onto a cell type from data --
// Divide, Grow, Photosynthesize. Deciding where to step is not that: it is
// creature.rs's own code, running from the active-site schedule, and there
// is nothing about it for a species to compose yet. What this file buys is
// everything *around* the movement -- a handle that goes stale safely, a
// cell list, a slot that comes back on death -- which is exactly the
// substrate the parallel scheme had reinvented badly.
//
// `Segment` is declared and unused: a worm is a one-cell chain. It is here
// because the ants that follow are 2-3 cells and a species file that
// declares only what it uses today would have to be edited in lockstep
// with them for no benefit.
(
    name: "worm",
    cell_types: [
        (Head, []),
        (Segment, []),
    ],
)
